{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel and take from them.</b> The verse's saying <b>Speak</b> etc. <b>and take from them</b> appears to be two matters, inasmuch as HaShem commands Israel to bring the staffs and Mosheh to take from them; therefore it says <b>Speak … and take</b>. And even though it did not mention Israel's bringing, it alluded to it through the power of the vav of <b>and take</b>. Further, a reason appears for its saying <b>and take</b> with the addition of the vav — to add to the first matter, besides the remembrance of the fire-pans which they made into hammered plates etc., further: <b>take from them</b> etc., to show them that in Aharon He chose for Himself, yah." "<b>From them etc. from all their princes.</b> The reason it was necessary to say <b>from them</b> and it did not suffice to say ״from all their princes״ — to establish that he should not take the staffs except from them, and that Mosheh should not take on their behalf. And if it had said ״take from their princes, staff by staff,״ I would not be obligated to say that they bring them, and I would say that it is equally valid for Mosheh to take on their behalf — and a person's agent is like him — or I would say that the intent of saying <b>from their princes</b> etc. is that it be from their property, but in any case Mosheh takes. Therefore the statement <b>from them</b> comes to restrict. And the reason for the matter is so that there be no room to say that Mosheh chose the wood for them and it would not succeed."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:46.911904192Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}